%I A264843 #30 Aug 20 2026 07:44:18
%S A264843 1,3,2,3,2,3,3,3,3,3,2,3,3,3,2,2,3,3,3,3,3,1,1,3,3,2,3,3,2,3,2,2,3,3,
%T A264843 3,3,3,3,3,3,3,2,1,1,3,3,2,3,1,1,3,3,3,3,3,2,2,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,
%U A264843 2,3,3,3,3,2,1,3,3,3,3,3,2,2,3,2,3,3,3
%N A264843 Maximal numbers of consecutive positive integers congruent to 1 modulo 3 that are all squarefree.
%C A264843 a(n) takes only values 1,2,3, since from every four numbers == 1 (mod 3), at least one is divisible by 4, hence nonsquarefree.
%C A264843 From _Amiram Eldar_, Aug 20 2026: (Start)
%C A264843 Let c_1 = 27/(4*Pi^2) = 0.683917989585779957246... (the asymptotic density of numbers k such that 3*k+1 is squarefree).
%C A264843 c_2 = (9/7) * Product_{p prime} (1 - 2/p^2) = 0.414815270064743147887... (the asymptotic density of numbers k such that 3*k+1 and 3*k+4 are both squarefree).
%C A264843 c_3 = (3/2) * Product_{p prime} (1 - 3/p^2) = 0.188230471358713947501... (the asymptotic density of numbers k such that 3*k+1, 3*k+4, and 3*k+7 are all squarefree).
%C A264843 Then, the asymptotic densities of the occurrences of 1, 2, and 3 in this sequence are (c_1-2*c_2+c_3)/(c_1-c_2) = 0.15799885222521438412..., (c_2-2*c_3)/(c_1-c_2) = 0.14252671773655912643..., and c_3/(c_1-c_2) = 0.69947443003822648944..., respectively.
%C A264843 The asymptotic mean of this sequence is c_1/(c_1 - c_2) = 2.54147557781301210531... . (End)
%H A264843 Amiram Eldar, <a href="/A264843/b264843.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a> (terms 1..1000 from Peter J. C. Moses)
%e A264843 From the first integers congruent to 1 (mod 3): 1, 4, 7, 10, 13, 16, 19, 22, 25, 28, 31, 34, 37, ..., the squarefree ones are (1), (7,10,13), (19,22), (31,34,37). So, a(1)=1, a(2)=3, a(3)=2, a(4)=3.
%t A264843 Map[Count[#,True]&,DeleteCases[Split[Map[SquareFreeQ[3#-2]&,Range[500]]],{___,False,___}]] (* _Peter J. C. Moses_, Nov 26 2015 *)
%o A264843 (PARI) lista(nn) = {nb = 0; for (n=0, nn, if (issquarefree(3*n+1), nb++, if (nb, print1(nb, ", ")); nb = 0););} \\ _Michel Marcus_, Dec 15 2015
